{"ts": 1789778956.5519822, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "Feature 29 as third leg (1.003) stands clearly above every weak alternative (0.79\u20130.85), consistent with 29 having real standalone signal (0.483) rather than winning by chance.\n\n**Calibrating my OOS forecast.** The in-sample 1.003 will shrink for two reasons:\n\n1. **Selection bias.** I ran ~65 evaluations and picked the max. But the winner was built from features that were independently strong *before* any pair/triple search, and its Sharpe matches the independent-combination prediction (1.013) almost exactly. This means little of the 1.003 is search-inflated interaction \u2014 the gain came from diversification, which is the most reliably persistent source of Sharpe. Selection bias is real but modest here.\n\n2. **Component shrinkage.** Each leg's standalone Sharpe (0.644, 0.618, 0.483) was itself the max-of-two-signs on a single sample, with SE \u2248 0.22. Even choosing signs correctly, true effects are likely somewhat smaller than measured \u2014 especially feature 29, the weakest leg. If true components are ~0.5/0.5/0.35, the true combination Sharpe is ~0.78.\n\nCentering on **0.78**, with generous uncertainty: sampling error alone is ~0.22, plus I'm unsure how much shrinkage applies and whether feature relationships are stable OOS. I'll use **sd = 0.30**, which puts meaningful probability on both a disappointing ~0.4 and a maintained ~1.0."}
