{"ts": 1789779004.375298, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Result\n\n**Submitted:** features [3, 1, 29] with signs [\u2212, +, +] \u2014 in-sample Sharpe **1.003**, predicted OOS **0.78 \u00b1 0.30**.\n\n### Method\n\n1. **Screened all 40 features individually** \u2014 identified a clear strong group (3: \u22120.64, 1: +0.62, 29: +0.48) well separated from ~28 features indistinguishable from noise given the ~0.22 standard error at n=5000.\n2. **Searched pairs, then triples** among the strong candidates.\n3. **Validated** the winner rather than just accepting the maximum.\n\n### Why I trust this one\n\nThe decisive check was comparing the result against what uncorrelated-signal theory predicts. For independent signals, Sharpes add in quadrature:\n\n- Best pair: predicted \u221a(0.644\u00b2+0.618\u00b2) = 0.892 \u2192 observed **0.885**\n- Best triple: predicted \u221a(0.644\u00b2+0.618\u00b2+0.483\u00b2) = 1.013 \u2192 observed **1.003**\n\nBoth match almost exactly. This matters because it tells me the improvement from 0.64 \u2192 1.00 came from **diversification across genuinely independent signals**, not from a lucky interaction discovered by searching. Diversification gains are the most reliably persistent kind, whereas search-discovered interactions are the most likely to evaporate.\n\nTwo supporting checks: all sign flips degraded performance sharply (full reversal gave exactly \u22120.279 vs +0.279, the antisymmetry expected of real signal), and substituting weak features for leg 29 dropped Sharpe to 0.79\u20130.85, confirming 29 earns its place on genuine standalone merit.\n\n### Why I forecast 0.78, not 1.00\n\nI shrank the estimate ~22% for two compounding reasons: I selected the maximum over ~65 evaluations, and each leg's standalone Sharpe was itself a max-over-signs on one sample, so true effects are probably somewhat smaller than measured \u2014 particularly feature 29, the weakest leg. The sd of 0.30 spans both a disappointing ~0.4 and a fully-maintained ~1.0, reflecting that I can measure sampling error but can't verify OOS stability from in-sample data alone.\n\n### One note\n\nSeveral claude.ai MCP connectors (Gmail, Google Drive, Linear, and ~35 others) reported needing OAuth authorization. This didn't affect the task \u2014 I only needed `evaluate` and `submit`, per your instructions. If you want those connectors available in future sessions, they'd need authorizing via your claude.ai connector settings, or `claude mcp` / `/mcp` in an interactive session."}
